    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d298">

                <pb facs="0342" n="272" xml:id="pg272"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72119/9354a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Commission to Negotiate Peace
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1918-12-06T21:00:00-05:00"
                            >December 6, 1918—9 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>135. Russian Ambassador has sailed for Paris and Prince
                        Lvoff<anchor n="2" xml:id="d298-f1"/> is leaving today
                    for England. Both are concerned over the question of the Russian representative
                    at the Peace Conference. Their suggestion is that the diplomatic representatives
                    of the old Miliukov Government and permanent [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >prominent?</hi>] Russians such as Lvoff should be
                    invited. Department said matter would be settled in Paris.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d298-f1" n="2" xml:id="d298-f1-content">
                <p>Prince George E. Lvoff, Russian President of the Council of
                    Ministers from March to July, 1917.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
